 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Complexity:** The encoding step is performed once per volume, making inference efficient for multiple transfer functions on the same volume [S1]. The concatenation operation is $O(1)$ relative to spatial resolution but adds to the input dimension of the decoder [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; beneficial during inference when multiple conditions (e.g., TFs) are applied to a single volume, as the expensive encoder pass is reused [S1].
-- **Limitations:** The condition is only directly visible to the first decoder layer; deeper layers must infer the condition from propagated features, which may limit expressiveness compared to weight-modulation methods [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Encoding is $O(V)$ where $V$ is volume size, done once. Decoding is $O(N)$ per sample, where $N$ is the number of evaluation points. Re-evaluation is required for every change in $z$ or $d$ [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the latent vector $z$ ($512$ floats per volume) [S1]. The decoder weights are shared [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; leverages standard conditional generative modeling techniques [S1]; efficient inference for multiple transfer functions on the same volume since $z$ is constant [S1].
+- **Limitations:** The condition is only directly visible to the first layer, potentially limiting the network's ability to use global information in deeper processing stages [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is applied in the context of deep volumetric ambient occlusion, where it is used to condition a generative network on both the input volume modality and the transfer function [S1]. It draws from conditional generative modeling practices [S1]. The general concept of latent code concatenation in Neural Fields is discussed in broader introductions to the field [S2, unofficial].
+Concatenation Conditioning is derived from standard practices in conditional generative modeling [S1]. It was applied to volumetric ambient occlusion by [S1] to condition illumination prediction on both volume modality and transfer function descriptors. The approach contrasts with more sophisticated injection strategies like Adaptive Instance Normalization (AdaIN) [S1].
 
